        'grammar-a1-2' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Be and Have Got: Basics',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThe verbs \"be\" and \"have got\" are among the first words you learn in English. \"Be\" describes who you are or what something is, and \"have got\" talks about things you own or possess. This lesson will help you use both correctly in everyday sentences.\n\n## How it works\nThe verb \"be\" has three forms: am (for I), is (for he/she/it), and are (for you/we/they). \"Have got\" shows possession and follows the same pattern: I have got, he has got, we have got. To make negatives, add not after the verb. For questions, move the verb to the start.\n> She is a doctor. / I have got two sisters.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"They is happy.\" \u2192 \"They are happy.\" (Match the subject with the correct form of be.)\n- \"He have got a new bike.\" \u2192 \"He has got a new bike.\" (Use has with he/she/it.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nDescribe yourself using both verbs: say what you are (your job or nationality) and what you have got (possessions or family members).",
