        'grammar-a1-3' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'Sentence Structure: Subject and Verb',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nEvery complete sentence in English needs at least two parts: a subject (who or what the sentence is about) and a verb (the action or state). Understanding this simple rule will help you build correct sentences from your very first day of learning English.\n\n## How it works\nThe subject comes first and is usually a noun or pronoun (I, she, the cat, my friend). The verb follows and tells us what the subject does or is. You can add an object after the verb to say what the subject acts upon. Without both a subject and a verb, you have a fragment, not a sentence.\n> The cat sleeps. / She reads a book.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"Under the table.\" \u2192 This is not a sentence because there is no subject or verb.\n- \"Him is my brother.\" \u2192 \"He is my brother.\" (Use subject pronouns: he, she, it, not him, her, it.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nLook around the room right now and write three complete sentences. Each one must have a subject and a verb, for example: \"The light is on.\"",
