        'grammar-a2-1' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Past Simple: Irregular Verbs',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThe past simple lets you talk about finished actions \u2014 what you did yesterday, last week, or last year. While regular verbs simply add -ed, irregular verbs change form entirely. Learning the most common irregular verbs is essential for describing your past.\n\n## How it works\nTo form the past simple of regular verbs, add -ed (walked, played, listened). Irregular verbs have unique past forms that you need to memorise: go \u2192 went, see \u2192 saw, eat \u2192 ate. To make negatives, use did not (didn\'t) plus the base form. Questions use Did + subject + base form.\n> Yesterday I went to the shops. / She ate lunch at noon.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"She eated lunch at noon.\" \u2192 \"She ate lunch at noon.\" (Irregular verbs do not take -ed.)\n- \"I goed to school.\" \u2192 \"I went to school.\" (The past of \"go\" is \"went\", not \"goed\".)\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite five sentences about what you did last weekend. Try to include at least three different irregular verbs such as went, saw, or had.",
        ],
        'grammar-a2-2' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Going To: Future Plans',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\n\"Going to\" is a simple and common way to talk about the future in English. You use it when you have already decided to do something (a plan) or when you can see evidence that something will happen. This lesson will help you talk about your plans with confidence.\n\n## How it works\nThe structure is: subject + am/is/are + going to + base verb. Use am going to for I, is going to for he/she/it, and are going to for you/we/they. Use this form for plans you have already made and for predictions based on what you can see right now.\n> I am going to visit my grandmother next weekend. / Look at those clouds \u2014 it is going to rain.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I going to visit my grandmother.\" \u2192 \"I am going to visit my grandmother.\" (You need the verb \"am\" before \"going to\".)\n- \"She will going to the cinema.\" \u2192 \"She is going to the cinema.\" (Do not mix will and going to.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four sentences about your plans for next month using \"going to\". Include at least one plan about food or travel.",
        ],
        'grammar-a2-3' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Comparatives and Superlatives',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nComparatives and superlatives let you compare things and people. A comparative says one thing is more than another (taller, bigger), and a superlative says something is the most in its group (the tallest, the biggest). These structures are essential for everyday descriptions.\n\n## How it works\nFor short adjectives (one syllable), add -er for the comparative and -est for the superlative: tall \u2192 taller \u2192 the tallest. For long adjectives (two or more syllables), use more and most: beautiful \u2192 more beautiful \u2192 the most beautiful. Some adjectives are irregular: good \u2192 better \u2192 the best.\n> London is bigger than Edinburgh. / This is the best book I have ever read.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"She is more tall than her sister.\" \u2192 \"She is taller than her sister.\" (Short adjectives use -er, not \"more\".)\n- \"He is the goodest player.\" \u2192 \"He is the best player.\" (\"Good\" is irregular \u2014 use \"best\" for the superlative.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nCompare two cities you know or two things you own. Write two comparative sentences and one superlative sentence.",
        ],
        'grammar-b1-1' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Present Perfect: Life Experiences',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThe present perfect connects the past to the present. You use it to talk about experiences in your life (without saying exactly when), situations that started in the past and continue now, and recent events with a result in the present. It is one of the trickiest but most useful tenses.\n\n## How it works\nThe form is: have/has + past participle. Use \"for\" with a period of time (for three years) and \"since\" with a specific point in time (since 2019). Use the present perfect with ever and never to talk about life experiences, and with just, already, or yet for recent actions.\n> I have lived here since 2019. / She has just finished her homework.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"I have went to Japan.\" \u2192 \"I have been to Japan.\" (The past participle of \"go\" is \"gone\", but \"been\" is used for visits.)\n- \"I am living here since 2019.\" \u2192 \"I have lived here since 2019.\" (Use the present perfect with \"since\", not the present continuous.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite five sentences about your life experiences using the present perfect with ever, never, or just. For example: \"I have never visited Italy.\"",
        ],
        'grammar-b1-2' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'First Conditional: Real Situations',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThe first conditional talks about real and likely situations in the future. If something happens (the condition), then something else will happen (the result). This structure is essential for giving warnings, making promises, and discussing possible outcomes.\n\n## How it works\nThe structure is: If + present simple, will + base verb. The if-clause uses the present simple tense even though it refers to the future. The main clause uses will + base verb to show the result. You can also use modals like can, might, or may in the main clause instead of will.\n> If it rains, we will stay at home. / If you study hard, you will pass the exam.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"If it will rain, we will stay at home.\" \u2192 \"If it rains, we will stay at home.\" (Never use \"will\" in the if-clause.)\n- \"If I will see her, I will say hello.\" \u2192 \"If I see her, I will say hello.\" (The present simple goes in the if-clause, not \"will\".)\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite four first conditional sentences about things that might happen this week. Use if + present simple in each one.",
        ],
        'grammar-b1-3' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Modals: Obligation and Advice',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nModal verbs like must, should, and have to help you express how necessary something is. Must shows strong personal obligation or prohibition. Should gives advice. Have to talks about rules imposed by someone else. Understanding the difference between these will make your English more precise.\n\n## How it works\nUse must for strong internal obligation (\"I must study harder\") and must not for prohibition (\"You must not smoke here\"). Use should for advice or recommendations (\"You should see a doctor\"). Use have to for external rules or laws (\"I have to wear a uniform at school\"). After modals, always use the base form of the verb.\n> You must wear a seatbelt in a car. / You should see a doctor if the pain continues.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"You must wear a uniform at school.\" \u2192 \"You have to wear a uniform at school.\" (Use \"have to\" for external rules, not \"must\".)\n- \"You must smoke here.\" \u2192 \"You must not smoke here.\" (\"Must\" without \"not\" means you must do it, which is the opposite of a prohibition.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite five sentences: two using must, two using should, and one using have to. Try to express a real rule, a piece of advice, and a personal obligation.",
        ],
        'grammar-b2-1' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Passive Voice: Present and Past',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThe passive voice shifts the focus from who does the action to what is done. You use it when the action or the object is more important than the person performing it, or when the doer is unknown or obvious. It is common in formal, scientific, and news writing.\n\n## How it works\nThe structure is: object + am/is/are/was/were + past participle. For the present passive, use am/is/are + past participle. For the past passive, use was/were + past participle. Add by + agent only when the doer is important. You can use the passive in any tense by changing the auxiliary verb.\n> The meal was prepared by the chef. / English is spoken in over fifty countries.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"The window was breaking by the boy.\" \u2192 \"The window was broken by the boy.\" (Use the past participle \"broken\", not the present participle \"breaking\".)\n- \"Electric cars is produced by the company.\" \u2192 \"Electric cars are produced by the company.\" (Match the auxiliary to the subject: cars = are.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nFind five active sentences in a news article and rewrite them in the passive voice. Decide in each case whether you need to include the \"by\" phrase.",
        ],
        'grammar-b2-2' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Reported Speech: Statements',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nReported speech lets you tell someone what another person said without quoting them exactly. When you report a statement, the tenses, pronouns, and time expressions usually shift back. Mastering these changes is key to fluency in both spoken and written English.\n\n## How it works\nUse the pattern: subject + said (that) + reported clause. Shift the tenses back: am/is \u2192 was, have finished \u2192 had finished, will \u2192 would. Change pronouns to match the new speaker. Shift time and place words: today \u2192 that day, tomorrow \u2192 the next day, here \u2192 there.\n> Direct: \"I am tired.\" \u2192 Reported: She said she was tired.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"He said he will come tomorrow.\" \u2192 \"He said he would come the next day.\" (Shift both the tense and the time expression.)\n- \"She said she is tired.\" \u2192 \"She said she was tired.\" (Use the past tense in the reported clause, not the present.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nTake five direct speech sentences and convert each one to reported speech. Remember to change the tense, pronouns, and any time words.",
        ],
        'grammar-b2-3' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Second and Third Conditionals',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThe second and third conditionals talk about imaginary or unreal situations. The second conditional describes things that are not true now (hypothetical present), while the third conditional describes things that did not happen in the past (imaginary past). Both are essential for expressing dreams, regrets, and hypothetical ideas.\n\n## How it works\nSecond conditional: If + past simple, would + base verb. Use this for unreal present situations (\"If I were rich, I would travel the world\"). Third conditional: If + past perfect, would have + past participle. Use this for unreal past situations (\"If I had studied, I would have passed\"). Note that in formal English, we use were (not was) for all subjects in the second conditional.\n> If I were you, I would accept the offer. / If she had left earlier, she would have caught the train.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"If I was rich, I would travel.\" \u2192 \"If I were rich, I would travel.\" (Formal English uses \"were\" for all subjects in the second conditional.)\n- \"If I had known, I will have attended.\" \u2192 \"If I had known, I would have attended.\" (Use \"would have\", not \"will have\", in the result clause.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite two second conditional sentences about your dreams and two third conditional sentences about past regrets. For example: \"If I had more free time, I would learn to play the guitar.\"",
        ],
        'grammar-c1-1' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Inversion for Emphasis',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nInversion is a powerful rhetorical device in which the normal subject\u2013verb order is reversed, usually after a negative or restrictive adverbial at the start of a sentence. It creates emphasis and is common in formal writing, speeches, and literature. Mastering inversion will make your English sound more sophisticated and expressive.\n\n## How it works\nWhen a sentence begins with a negative adverbial such as never, rarely, seldom, hardly, no sooner, or not only, the auxiliary verb comes before the subject. For tenses without an auxiliary, add do/does/did. Important: positive adverbs like always and often do NOT trigger inversion.\n> Never had I seen such a beautiful sunset. / Rarely do we find such dedication.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"Always have I believed in equality.\" \u2192 \"Always\" is positive and does not trigger inversion; keep normal word order.\n- \"Never I had seen such a thing.\" \u2192 \"Never had I seen such a thing.\" (The auxiliary verb must come before the subject after the negative adverbial.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nRewrite four sentences beginning with Never, Rarely, Not only, and No sooner. Make sure you place the auxiliary verb before the subject in each one.",
        ],
        'grammar-c1-2' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Cleft Sentences for Focus',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nCleft sentences restructure a sentence to put focus on a particular element \u2014 the person, thing, time, or place you want to highlight. There are two main types: it-clefts (\"It was X who...\") and wh-clefts (\"What I need is...\"). They are a hallmark of advanced, polished English.\n\n## How it works\nIt-clefts follow the pattern: It + was/is + focused element + who/that + rest of sentence. Wh-clefts use: What/Where/When + clause + is/was + focused element. It-clefts emphasise a noun or phrase; wh-clefts emphasise the complement. Both types are common in academic writing, journalism, and speeches.\n> It was the manager who made the final decision. / What I need is more time.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"It was John who broke it the window.\" \u2192 \"It was John who broke the window.\" (Do not duplicate the object.)\n- \"That I need is more time.\" \u2192 \"What I need is more time.\" (Use \"what\" for wh-clefts, not \"that\".)\n\n## Try it yourself\nRewrite four simple sentences using cleft constructions to emphasise different parts. For example, turn \"Sarah solved the problem\" into both an it-cleft and a wh-cleft.",
        ],
        'grammar-c1-3' => ['skill' => 'grammar', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Nominalisation in Formal Writing',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nNominalisation is the process of turning verbs and adjectives into nouns (decide \u2192 decision, analyse \u2192 analysis). It is a key feature of academic and formal writing because it allows you to pack more information into a sentence and create an objective, impersonal tone. This skill is vital for university essays, reports, and professional documents.\n\n## How it works\nAdd common suffixes to convert verbs into nouns: -tion/-sion (decide \u2192 decision), -ment (implement \u2192 implementation), -ance/-ence (perform \u2192 performance). For adjectives, try -ness (weak \u2192 weakness) or -ity (diverse \u2192 diversity). Nominalised forms often need an article (the/a) and a preposition (of) before them.\n> The government made the decision to raise taxes. / The implementation of the new policy has been widely debated.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- \"The decide of the government was surprising.\" \u2192 \"The decision of the government was surprising.\" (Use the noun form, not the verb.)\n- \"We did the analyse of the data.\" \u2192 \"We did the analysis of the data.\" (Use the nominalised noun form after an article.)\n\n## Try it yourself\nTake five informal sentences with plain verbs and rewrite them using nominalised forms. For example, change \"They decided quickly\" to \"Their quick decision surprised everyone.\"",
        ],

        // ── Reading (15) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        'reading-a1-1' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'A Postcard from the Beach',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nAt A1 level, reading means understanding very short, simple texts about everyday life. In this lesson you will practise finding basic information such as names, times, and actions in a short postcard. Being able to pick out key details is the foundation of all reading skills.\n\n## How it works\nWhen you read a short text at this level, look for specific details that answer the question directly. The information is usually stated plainly \u2014 you do not need to guess or infer. Read the question first, then scan the text for the matching detail such as a time, a number, or a name.\n> Maria wakes up at seven o\'clock. She eats breakfast at half past seven. Then she walks to school.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Not reading the whole text before answering \u2014 some details appear later in the passage and you might miss them.\n- Confusing similar details, such as the time Maria wakes up (seven o\'clock) with the time she eats breakfast (half past seven).\n\n## Try it yourself\nRead the text once more and underline every piece of information that includes a time or a number. Then check your answers against the text.",
        ],
        'reading-a1-2' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'My Family and I',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nReading a simple family description teaches you to identify people, their roles, ages, and likes. This is a key everyday skill \u2014 understanding who people are and what they enjoy. At A1, the text gives you all the answers directly, so your job is to locate them quickly.\n\n## How it works\nShort descriptive texts present facts in a clear order: name, job, age, interests. Look for numbers (ages), job words (doctor, teacher), and likes (likes, enjoys). Each sentence usually gives one new piece of information about a person, so read carefully from start to finish.\n> Mr Green is a doctor. Mrs Green is a teacher. They have two children: Tom is eight and Lily is five.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Confusing details between people, such as thinking Lily likes dinosaurs when it is actually Tom who does.\n- Miscounting family members when the text lists them with descriptions attached to each name.\n\n## Try it yourself\nAfter reading, list every person mentioned in the text with their age and what they like. Check your list against the passage to make sure nothing is mixed up.",
        ],
        'reading-a1-3' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'A1', 'title' => 'A Visit to the Supermarket',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThis lesson practises scanning a short descriptive text to find specific facts: what something looks like, where it is, what it sells, and when it opens and closes. Finding these details quickly is a useful real-life skill for reading signs, notices, and short descriptions.\n\n## How it works\nWhen a question asks about a specific detail, scan the text for the keyword in the question. For example, if the question asks about colour, look for colour words. If it asks about time, look for numbers and time expressions. Do not assume information that is not written in the text.\n> The shop sells books and pens. It is open from nine in the morning to five in the afternoon. The shop is blue.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Guessing information not stated in the text, such as assuming the shop sells food because most shops do.\n- Mixing up opening and closing times when the text gives both in the same sentence.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite down three facts about Anna\'s shop: its colour, what it sells, and its opening hours. Check each answer against the text to make sure they are correct.",
        ],
        'reading-a2-1' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'A Holiday in the Countryside',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThis lesson helps you follow a sequence of events across different days. At A2, texts often describe a series of activities using time markers like Monday, Tuesday, and so on. Being able to match an event to the right day is an important reading skill for diaries, itineraries, and stories.\n\n## How it works\nRead for time markers first \u2014 words like Monday, Tuesday, On Wednesday. Each time marker signals a new event. When a question asks about a specific day, go directly to that part of the text and read the sentence carefully. Pay attention to cause words like \"so\" which explain why something happened.\n> On Monday we visited the beach. On Tuesday we went to a fish restaurant. On Wednesday we walked along the coast path.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Mixing up activities that happen on different days, such as thinking the beach visit happened on Tuesday instead of Monday.\n- Missing the word \"so\" which explains cause and effect: \"It rained on Thursday, so we stayed inside.\"\n\n## Try it yourself\nDraw a simple table with the days of the week and write one activity next to each day mentioned in the text. This helps you organise information clearly.",
        ],
        'reading-a2-2' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'Daily Life in London',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nNews-style texts report facts clearly and concisely: numbers, places, causes, and solutions. This lesson teaches you to read a short news item and identify the key factual details. Understanding cause and effect in texts is a step up from A1 and prepares you for more complex reading.\n\n## How it works\nScan for factual keywords: numbers (forty minutes), place names (Manchester, London), and cause words (because, due to, caused by). News texts usually put the most important fact in the first sentence. Read the first sentence carefully to get the main idea, then check the rest for details.\n> The nine-fifteen train from Manchester to London was delayed by forty minutes this morning.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Confusing the cause of the delay with the solution offered to passengers (engineering work caused it; a replacement bus was the solution).\n- Missing the exact number when the text embeds it in a longer phrase like \"delayed by forty minutes.\"\n\n## Try it yourself\nFind three factual details in the text: one number, one place name, and one reason. Write each one in a complete sentence.",
        ],
        'reading-a2-3' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'A2', 'title' => 'A Train Journey to Edinburgh',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nSome reading questions at A2 ask you to go beyond simple facts and make a reasonable inference based on clues in the text. This lesson teaches you to read between the lines: when a text says someone is \"thinking about\" something, you can often predict what they will do next.\n\n## How it works\nRead the text and look for clues about preferences and intentions. Words like \"thinking about\", \"wants to\", and \"enjoys\" give you evidence for predictions. When a question asks what someone will probably do, combine two or more clues to form a logical answer.\n> Emma is thinking about a cookbook. Her mother enjoys cooking Italian food. The clues together suggest she will buy the cookbook.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Confusing what someone is considering with what they have already decided \u2014 the text says Emma \"is thinking about\" the cookbook, not that she has bought it.\n- Selecting an answer that is true but does not answer the specific question asked.\n\n## Try it yourself\nPredict what Emma will buy and write one sentence explaining your prediction using evidence from the text, such as \"She will probably buy the cookbook because her mother enjoys cooking.\"",
        ],
        'reading-b1-1' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Opinions on Education',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nAt B1 you start reading opinion-based texts where the writer presents an argument and a solution. This lesson teaches you to identify the main concern of the writer and the recommendation they make. Distinguishing between the problem and the proposed solution is a critical reading skill.\n\n## How it works\nLook for signal words that indicate opinion: argues, suggests, believes, claims. The problem is usually stated early in the text, and the solution follows. Read the whole text before answering so you can separate the writer\'s personal view from factual evidence they cite.\n> The writer argues that people should set strict daily limits on their screen time.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Confusing the problem (excessive use increases loneliness) with the solution (set strict daily limits on screen time).\n- Selecting a supporting detail (social media helps us stay in touch) as the main concern when it is actually a counterpoint.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite one sentence summarising the writer\'s main concern and one sentence summarising their suggested solution. Keep each to ten words or fewer.",
        ],
        'reading-b1-2' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'The Benefits of Volunteering',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nShort opinion passages present a main idea supported by details. This lesson trains you to pick out the writer\'s central recommendation from the supporting evidence around it. At B1, questions may ask you what the writer \"suggests\" or \"recommends\", so you need to locate the specific advice.\n\n## How it works\nThe main recommendation often appears in the final sentence of a short passage. Read the whole text, then identify which sentence is the writer\'s direct suggestion (look for verbs like suggests, recommends, advises). Supporting details explain why the suggestion matters but are not the suggestion itself.\n> The writer suggests that even one hour a week can make a real difference to your community.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Selecting a supporting detail (volunteering helps you meet people) as the writer\'s suggestion when the actual suggestion is to volunteer.\n- Confusing what people generally do with what the writer specifically recommends.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite the writer\'s main suggestion in one sentence. Then list two supporting reasons the writer gives for why it matters.",
        ],
        'reading-b1-3' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'B1', 'title' => 'Reading for Pleasure',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nThis lesson helps you distinguish between facts and opinions in a text. At B1, opinion pieces mix objective information with the writer\'s personal views. Recognising which statements are facts and which are the writer\'s opinions is essential for critical reading.\n\n## How it works\nFactual statements can be checked and proven (\"Many people spend several hours a day on social media\"). Opinions reflect the writer\'s view (\"studies suggest that excessive use can increase feelings of loneliness\"). Look for hedging language such as suggests, may, can, and argues, which signal opinions rather than hard facts.\n> While these platforms help us stay in touch with friends, studies suggest that excessive use can increase feelings of loneliness.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Treating a factual detail (social media helps us stay in touch) as the writer\'s opinion when it is presented as a generally accepted fact.\n- Ignoring contrast words like \"while\" and \"yet\" which signal that the writer is about to present a different perspective.\n\n## Try it yourself\nFind three statements in the text: one that is a clear fact, one that is clearly the writer\'s opinion, and one that uses hedging language. Label each one.",
        ],
        'reading-b2-1' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Urbanisation and Its Consequences',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nAt B2, academic texts present claims and support them with concrete evidence such as statistics, examples, and case studies. This lesson teaches you to separate the author\'s main argument from the evidence used to back it up. Recognising evidence types strengthens your analytical reading.\n\n## How it works\nThe main claim usually appears in the opening or closing sentences. Supporting evidence includes statistics (\"tripled in thirty years\"), named examples (\"Lagos\"), and lists of consequences. Words like \"for example\", \"such as\", and \"cites\" signal that evidence follows. Read the whole paragraph to see how the evidence connects to the claim.\n> The author cites the example of Lagos, where the population has tripled in thirty years, straining infrastructure beyond capacity.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Treating a specific example (Lagos) as the main argument when it is only evidence supporting a broader claim about urbanisation.\n- Missing hedging or qualification in the claim, which may use words like \"often\" or \"can lead to\" rather than absolute statements.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite the writer\'s main claim about urbanisation in one sentence. Then write the specific evidence from Lagos in a second sentence. Check that the second supports the first.",
        ],
        'reading-b2-2' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'The Ethics of Modern Technology',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nMany B2 texts present balanced arguments with multiple viewpoints and an underlying tension. This lesson trains you to identify the competing sides in an argument and to locate the specific tension the author highlights. Understanding how authors structure balanced arguments is key to advanced reading.\n\n## How it works\nLook for contrast signals: yet, however, nevertheless, although, while, tension, raises questions. These words signal that the author is presenting two sides. The tension is the central conflict between those sides. Read carefully to determine which disadvantage or concern the text emphasises as the main issue.\n> Artificial intelligence promises to revolutionise healthcare, yet it raises significant ethical questions.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Selecting only the positive side (AI will revolutionise healthcare) as the text\'s position when the author is actually highlighting the ethical tension.\n- Choosing an answer that is mentioned in the text but is not the main disadvantage the author describes.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite one sentence explaining the tension the author describes. Identify which side the author seems more concerned about and explain why using evidence from the text.",
        ],
        'reading-b2-3' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'B2', 'title' => 'Formal and Informal Registers',
            'content' => '## What you\'ll learn\nRegister refers to the level of formality in language. This lesson explores hedging language \u2014 expressions like \'it could be argued\' and \'the evidence suggests\' that soften claims. Understanding register and hedging is essential for academic reading and for distinguishing confident assertions from cautious ones.\n\n## How it works\nIn academic writing, authors use hedging to show they are not making absolute claims. Look for expressions with modal verbs (could, may, might) and tentative verbs (suggests, indicates, implies). Contrast these with direct assertions like \'It is a fact that\' or \'Everyone knows that\', which are more common in informal texts.\n> Expressions such as \'it could be argued\' signal that the writer is not making an absolute statement.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Treating all writing as equally certain, when hedging language deliberately signals uncertainty and caution.\n- Confusing the example of hedging (\'it could be argued\') with the author\'s own position on hedging, which is that it is appropriate in academic contexts.\n\n## Try it yourself\nFind two examples of hedging language in the text and rewrite each sentence without the hedge. Compare the two versions to see how the tone changes.',
        ],
        'reading-c1-1' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Academic Research Methodologies',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nAt C1, academic texts use complex sentence structures, abstract vocabulary, and nuanced argumentation. This lesson teaches you to engage with a text about the philosophy of science, identifying the main thesis, the counter-argument, and how the author qualifies their position. This level of reading demands sustained concentration and inference.\n\n## How it works\nLook for the author\'s thesis (the main claim) and then the qualification introduced by contrast markers like yet, however, or nevertheless. Abstract nouns (epistemological, falsifiability) may be unfamiliar but can often be decoded from context. The author\'s position is usually revealed not in the opening claim but in how they evaluate the counter-argument.\n> Popper\'s criterion remains influential, yet critics contend that in practice scientists often retain theories despite anomalous findings.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Confusing the position being described (Popper\'s falsifiability) with the author\'s own evaluation of it.\n- Missing the qualification: the text says the criterion \"remains influential\" but also that critics challenge its practical application.\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite one sentence summarising the author\'s overall position on falsifiability. Make sure your summary captures both the influence and the criticism described in the text.",
        ],
        'reading-c1-2' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'The Politics of Language',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nC1 reading requires you to evaluate competing research claims and identify the degree of certainty in each. This lesson about bilingualism and cognition teaches you to recognise hedged findings (\"compelling evidence\", \"remains contested\") and to understand that scientific conclusions are often provisional rather than definitive.\n\n## How it works\nPay close attention to hedging and qualification: words like compelling, contested, modest, and may. These signal how confident the research community is about a finding. A text that uses both \"compelling evidence\" and \"remains contested\" is presenting a balanced, cautious position rather than a settled conclusion.\n> Some longitudinal studies report modest effects that diminish over time, suggesting that earlier findings may have overstated the benefits.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Treating the initial positive claim (\"compelling evidence\") as the final conclusion, when the text goes on to qualify and challenge it.\n- Selecting \"The benefits are always strong\" when the text specifically says effects are \"modest\" and \"diminish over time.\"\n\n## Try it yourself\nWrite one sentence explaining why the cognitive benefits of bilingualism are contested. Use at least one hedging expression from the text in your answer.",
        ],
        'reading-c1-3' => ['skill' => 'reading', 'level' => 'C1', 'title' => 'Critical Thinking in the Sciences',
            'content' => "## What you\'ll learn\nAt C1, authors often qualify their central claim within the same sentence, using structures like \"While X, Y\" or \"X, yet Y\". This lesson teaches you to read with full attention to qualifications and to understand that a single sentence can hold both a positive claim and its limitation. This is essential for critical academic reading.\n\n## How it works\nThe author acknowledges a benefit and then immediately introduces a drawback using contrast signals: while, simultaneously, yet, although. Read the entire sentence before forming an opinion \u2014 the qualification often comes in the second half. Identify what the author celebrates and what they caution against.\n> While the democratisation of knowledge through open-access publishing is widely celebrated, it simultaneously poses challenges for scholarly quality control.\n\n## Common mistakes at this level\n- Reading only the first clause (\"democratisation of knowledge is widely celebrated\") and missing the qualification in the second half of the sentence.\n- Selecting an answer that captures only one side of the argument when the author is presenting a nuanced, dual perspective.\n\n## Try it yourself\nIdentify how the author qualifies the claim about open-access publishing. Write one sentence that captures both the benefit and the challenge in your own words.",
        ],

